                <h2>Lead with the Conclusion: Appeal Success Depends on "Evidence Match"</h2>
<p>When optimizers receive an account suspension notice, their first reaction is often to submit a large volume of materials, believing "more materials show more sincerity." However, based on the dozens of appeal cases we've handled, <strong>appeal approval rates are essentially unrelated to the quantity of materials—what truly matters is the match between evidence and the suspension reason.</strong></p>
<p>Google Ads officially labels specific reason codes in suspension notices. Appeal materials must focus on these codes, not submitting a "universal template."</p>
<hr />
<h2>Four Key Steps for Preparing Appeal Materials</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Identify the Actual Suspension Reason</h3>
<p>Common account suspension reasons fall into three categories:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Payment-related issues</strong> (invalid/expired credit cards; billing threshold anomalies)</li>
<li><strong>Policy violations</strong> (brand name infringement, misleading content, sensitive category promotions)</li>
<li><strong>Invalid activity detection</strong> (abnormal click/display data flagged as artificial traffic)</li>
</ol>
<blockquote>
<p>Tip: After receiving the suspension email, first verify the reason code (such as GMBL-001, CBC-004). This is ten times more efficient than guessing the cause.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>Step 2: Prepare Evidence, Not Justifications</h3>
<p>Many teams' appeals stay at the level of "we promise it won't happen again"—precisely the lowest-success-rate approach. The Google Ads review team needs <strong>objective, verifiable evidence:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Payment issues: Provide valid credit card statements, screenshots of new payment methods</li>
<li>Policy violations: Provide modification records of actual website content, landing page URL comparisons</li>
<li>Invalid activity: Provide third-party traffic analysis reports (such as Google Analytics traffic quality screenshots)</li>
</ul>
<p>A <strong>certain utility app team</strong> was suspended for "invalid activity." They submitted three months of GA data screenshots and server log summaries, and their appeal succeeded within two weeks. The key was that they didn't make excuses—they used data to prove traffic authenticity.</p>
<h3>Step 3: Write a Concise Appeal Statement</h3>
<p>Appeal letter structure recommended:</p>
<ol>
<li>Account ID and problem description (one sentence)</li>
<li>Corrective measures already taken (specific actions)</li>
<li>Evidence attachment list</li>
<li>Commitments and subsequent monitoring plans</li>
</ol>
<p>Keep it within one page. The focus is helping reviewers quickly locate key information.</p>
<h3>Step 4: Submit Through the Correct Channel</h3>
<ul>
<li>Manager Account suspension: Use the "Request Review" portal within the account</li>
<li>Payment issues: Contact Google Ads support first, explaining new account migration or payment method updates</li>
<li>Urgent situations: Submit supplementary explanations through the Google Business Profile backend</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Two High-Frequency Pitfall Reminders</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Do not resubmit the same materials repeatedly</strong>: The system flags duplicate submissions, and intervals under 72 hours may result in automatic rejection.</li>
<li><strong>Do not open multiple new accounts simultaneously</strong>: Opening sub-accounts after a main account suspension constitutes "circumvention behavior" and will directly result in associated account suspensions.</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Summary</h2>
<p>Account suspension isn't the end—appeals are a standard recourse process. The core principle is simple: <strong>let evidence speak, not sympathy-seeking promises.</strong> Before submitting, ask yourself: "Can reviewers independently determine the problem has been resolved after viewing these materials?" If the answer is "yes," your appeal success rate will increase significantly.</p>
